{"name":"Heliotropic Mauve","hex":"#9187bd","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"heliotropic","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Exhibiting heliotropism."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/heliotropic"},{"term":"mauve","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A rich purple synthetic dye, which faded easily, briefly popular c. 1859‒1873 and now called mauveine.","A pale purple or violet colour, like the colour of the dye after it has faded."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a pale purple colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mauve"}],"entries":[{"title":"Heliotropism","blocks":[{"text":"Heliotropism, a form of tropism, is the diurnal or seasonal motion of plant parts in response to the direction of the Sun."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Bellis_perennis_-_Gaenseblume4.jpg/330px-Bellis_perennis_-_Gaenseblume4.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heliotropism","wikidata":"Q1602587"},{"title":"Mauve","blocks":[{"text":"Mauve is a pale purple color named after the mallow flower. The first use of the word mauve as a color was in 1796–1798 according to the Oxford English Dictionary, but its use seems to have been rare before 1859. Another name for the color is mallow, with the first recorded use of mallow as a color name in English in 1611."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Wilde_Malve.JPG/330px-Wilde_Malve.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mauve","wikidata":"Q604079"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/SunflowerSunset2.jpg/500px-SunflowerSunset2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/SunflowerSunset2.jpg/1280px-SunflowerSunset2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/SunflowerSunset2.jpg/500px-SunflowerSunset2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sunflowers in full bloom are not heliotropic, so they do not follow the Sun. The flowerheads face east all day, so in the afternoon, they are backlit by the Sun.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASunflowerSunset2.jpg"}],"sounds":{"mauve":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/05/En-uk-mauve.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/0/05/En-uk-mauve.ogg/En-uk-mauve.ogg.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a8/En-us-mauve.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/a/a8/En-us-mauve.ogg/En-us-mauve.ogg.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/ce/En-us-mauve2.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/c/ce/En-us-mauve2.ogg/En-us-mauve2.ogg.mp3"}]}}}